---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PostLifecycle from '../components/PostLifecycle.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Post to LinkedIn from your terminal"
  description="postiee posts to LinkedIn's official API and keeps a searchable local history, because LinkedIn will not let any API read your posts back."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">LinkedIn CLI for people who use terminals</p>
  <h1>Post to LinkedIn. Search your own history.</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    LinkedIn's permission to read your own posts is
    <strong>closed to every applicant</strong> — not slow, not paid, closed.
    postiee captures the post ID the moment it is created and keeps a local,
    full-text-searchable record, because that record is the only copy that will
    ever exist.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="cta" href="https://github.com/aswathas/postiee">Get it on GitHub</a>
    <a class="cta secondary" href="/docs">Read the docs</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>

  <p><strong>macOS and Linux</strong></p>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aswathas/postiee/main/install.sh | sh</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Windows (PowerShell)</strong></p>
  <pre><code>irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aswathas/postiee/main/install.ps1 | iex</code></pre>

  <p>
    No Go toolchain needed — those download a prebuilt binary. Full setup,
    including the LinkedIn app you need to create, is on the
    <a href="/docs">docs page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Use it</h2>

  <pre><code># post something
$ postiee post "shipped a new feature today"

# with an image
$ postiee post "our signup curve" --image chart.png --alt "signups by week"

# find something you posted months ago
$ postiee search harness

# everything you've posted, newest first
$ postiee history</code></pre>

  <h2>What it does, and what it cannot</h2>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="badge ok">works</span></p>
      <p>
        <strong>Post</strong> text, one image, or a file. Public, connections,
        or logged-in visibility.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="badge ok">works</span></p>
      <p>
        <strong>Search</strong> your own posts with stemming and multi-term
        queries. 145&nbsp;µs across 1,000 posts.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="badge no">impossible</span></p>
      <p>
        <strong>Comments, reactions, analytics</strong> — need LinkedIn partner
        approval this project does not have.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <p><span class="badge no">impossible</span></p>
      <p>
        <strong>Messaging, invitations</strong> — no public LinkedIn API exists
        at any access tier.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    Those limits are not oversights. They are why this tool is small and why the
    local history is the point. <a href="/features">See the full scope
    breakdown</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The whole idea, in one diagram</h2>

  <figure>
    <PostLifecycle />
    <figcaption>
      Every call <code>postiee post</code> makes, and the three places naive
      LinkedIn clients break.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Fast, because there is very little to do</h2>

  <table>
    <caption class="sr-only" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px">
      Measured operation latency
    </caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Operation</th>
        <th scope="col" class="num">Latency</th>
        <th scope="col" class="num">Allocations</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Search 1,000 posts</td>
        <td class="num">145 µs</td>
        <td class="num">32</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Search 10,000 posts</td>
        <td class="num">347 µs</td>
        <td class="num">32</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>List history</td>
        <td class="num">79 µs</td>
        <td class="num">581</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Record a post</td>
        <td class="num">1.48 ms</td>
        <td class="num">33</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Search is nearly flat in corpus size: 10× the posts costs 2.4× the latency,
    because FTS5 queries an index rather than scanning rows. Reproduce with
    <code>go test -bench=. -benchmem ./bench/</code>.
  </p>
</Base>